comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-20T22:09:48.389Z","harness:claude-code","Sentry PM-CLI-40 and PM-CLI-41 (TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading 'ts'); release 2026.11.20, environment automation, 4 events each between 17:15 and 17:18Z on 2026-11-20) came from the deliberate reproduction behind this item: the released build and a development build, each run against a tracker with a null history line. Both were annotated and resolved so the reproduction cannot block the release Sentry gate. They are the stack evidence for this defect, and a real user hitting it will regress them. Future reproductions must set PM_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1 and PM_SENTRY_DISABLED=1."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-21T01:25:23.396Z","harness:claude-code","Reader census (AC3), 2026-11-21. The shared decoder decodeHistorySnapshot (src/core/history/read.ts) feeds readHistorySnapshot, readIdentifiedHistorySnapshot and readHistoryEntries, which about 20 modules call: workspace-history, item-transaction, query/history, query/activity, query/get (claim state), ownership-source, history-read, governance/stale-work, governance/closure-timestamps, event-index, workspace-transaction, merge/receipts, lifecycle/restore, lifecycle/item-revision, lifecycle/checkpoint-rollback, history/maintenance (repair, redact, compact), governance/assurance and assurance-runtime, tombstone and runtime-primitives. All of them now get the typed refusal history_record_invalid (item_id, reason such as \"a JSON null\", details.line) instead of a reader dereferencing null.ts.\n\nReaders with their own line parser keep their behaviour. drift-scan (pm health / validate --check-history-drift) still counts the stream under unreadable_streams. storage-integrity still reports the line as unparseable. merge/three-way still refuses non-objects. history-storage-stats, provenance-health, health (tombstone classification, merge receipt references, telemetry state), author-attribution and merge/reconcile all skip or catch non-object values already. Salvage has its own decoder and now shares the decoder's predicate.\n\nScope decision: the decoder refuses only non-object values (null, boolean, number, string, array). A first draft also required string ts, author, op, before_hash, after_hash and an array patch on every object. It failed 26 specs because legacy streams and fixtures legitimately carry objects without author or without a patch array (one spec renders compact history for legacy entries with missing patch arrays). Objects keep each reader's existing tolerance.\n\nRecovery (AC2): history-repair --salvage-tail now removes a line whose JSON value is a null, a boolean or a number wherever it sits, since such a value cannot hold a record, and keeps every record byte-identical. The receipt gains non_record_lines. A string, array or incomplete object might encode a record, so salvage still refuses those. Executed in tests/integration/history-bridge.integration.spec.ts: a null line followed by a later pm write is salvaged, after which activity and health --strict-exit pass. Known limit: salvage still needs the retained records to verify, so a stream with both a scalar line and a chain break has no pm-native recovery (the decoder blocks the repair). Recovering that case means restoring the stream from version control."
